const GROUND = `
PROJECT (root ${ROOT}; paths relative to it). The architecture of PLAT (an original settlement map generator and the SettlementForge map layer) is now a SET: the base docs/ARCHITECTURE.md (with docs/ARCHITECTURE-RATIONALE.md, whose Part A holds the full judgment calls), the twelve design volumes docs/volumes/V01-…V12-*.md at build depth for every staged subject, docs/volumes/INDEX.md (the product map and the combined build plan), and ${FFILE} (the volumes' base change requests, numbered from 200, NOT yet applied — the volumes were designed as if they were). The owner (2026-09-28): "make sure you do a complete coherence and cohesive pass as well" and "both with the dossier and all of the interconnecting pieces making sure that every piece of engineering is to be wired to something else". This is that pass: a COHERENCE, COHESION AND WIRING pass over the whole set, not a redesign. The owner added (2026-09-28 ~23:00): "in the eventual coherence pass, also integrate elegance into it as well where appropriate but not lowering nuance" — so it is also an ELEGANCE pass under principle (9). ALSO APPLY docs/AMENDMENTS-2026-09-28g.md (OWNER RULE items numbered from 102 that reached the program after the bundled pass; the owner's words are the authority): treat every g item exactly as an f item, EXCEPT that g items from 111 onward reached the program after the volumes pass had integrated: each is applied by EVERY unit it names — the base parts and the named volumes alike — as new design at build depth (records, rules, constants, tests, lifecycle rows), a volume's part written by that volume's editor as an addition, never as a change of another decision; the canon routes each part, and a verifier raises F-MISSING where a named part is absent; beyond that,  — routed by the canon to the unit that applies it, applied as ruled (a design volume that already carries its part is cited, never restated), verified as F-MISSING if absent.
PRINCIPLES: (1) SINGLE SOURCE ACROSS THE SET — every normative fact has exactly one canonical home: the base owns every mechanism shared by more than one stage and everything the base builds; a volume owns what only its stage builds; everywhere else the fact is referenced ("see §x.y" for the base, "see V04 §3.2" for a volume), never restated. (2) ONE TERM PER CONCEPT — a glossary with one name for each concept; retired synonyms replaced everywhere (the canon lists them). (3) NOTHING LOST OR CHANGED — every inventoried item survives with the same meaning, except (a) the ${FFILE} items applied to the base as written (each is a ruled design change), (b) a conflict resolved as the canon rules, (c) an ELEGANCE ruling of the canon (principle (9)), which changes the design's FORM and never its behaviour; the order of precedence for a conflict is: the owner's rules (docs/OWNER-INSTRUCTIONS-2026-09-25.md, the OWNER RULE items of the amendments files, docs/DEFINITION.md) > an f item or a volumes integration decision (J-VI rows) that deliberately changes the base > the base's canonical home > a volume's restatement. (4) COHESION — the set reads as one design: the one-page summary, §1, §14 (packages: every volume's packages placed), §15 (matrices) and §16 (the build plan) agree with INDEX.md and with every volume's build plan; the one context vector of the plot model, prosperity field, granularity and greenery is the same object everywhere; the evolution, dossier and editing rules agree between the base and every volume; every test id (T-…, T-V…) and decision id (D-…, J-…, J-V…) is unique across the set; every cross-reference resolves (base → volume, volume → base, volume → volume). (5) PLAIN AND SHORT (with principle (9)'s shrink goal) — narration of how the text evolved removed ("round 3 added …", "per VD2-10 …"; the validation records keep it), restatement cut, compact prose; no target size, but report it. (6) The standing rules hold: no deferral wording ("TBD", "open", "later" as an unresolved choice); emulation-first defaults; the realism tiebreaker; clean-room (no reference code, identifier, grammar, palette, word list or table; §17.1); STANDALONE (nothing outside ${ROOT}; nothing from partitioned/ except its README); the dossier is the sole immutable truth (items 20, 20 (h), 22). (7) WIRED — every piece of engineering is connected to something else, both ways: every record and field is written by a named producer (a stage, an edit, the dossier port, a derivation row) and read by a named consumer (a stage, a renderer, an export, the reader or clerk, a test); every stage's outputs are consumed and its inputs produced; every rule or content table is read by a mechanism; every interface has an implementer and a caller; every edit kind has its tool (item 101), its lifecycle row (§7.4) and its undo; every tool maps to events; every package has consumers and its dependencies are declared (§14); every hook has both ends (the base side and the stage or volume that uses it); every test targets a mechanism and every mechanism that can fail has a test; every entity kind is rendered, labelled where it should be, exported and persisted; THE DOSSIER END TO END — every SettlementForge dossier field is consumed by a named mechanism or listed on the ignore-list (item 86), every derived map fact traces to its dossier inputs and its derivation row (item 20 (h)), and every dossier change reaches every fact that depends on it through the change envelope, the epoch or event fold and least-disruption mutation (items 22, 75, 76). An orphan (produced, never consumed), a dangling input (consumed, never produced), a hook with one end, or an unreached dossier field is a defect: this pass wires it (names the missing link, at its home) or removes the piece with a recorded ruling — never leaves it floating. OWNER RULING J121 (2026-09-29): the map is a SettlementForge module, so the volumes' owner questions and any design text about hosting and web origin, accounts, plans and save limits, backups, the preview host, the product's public name, the licence for users' maps, interface languages, the AI key or relay, the assistant and its model tier FOLLOW SETTLEMENTFORGE's existing product decisions: mark each such question answered as 'follows SettlementForge (J121)' and have the design read SettlementForge's decision as an input, never re-decide it; OWNER DELEGATION J122 (2026-09-29 ~08:00: "With the exceptional fable, I leave all decisions to you"): every remaining owner question in the volumes is DECIDED as recorded in docs/OWNER-DECISIONS-DELEGATED-2026-09-29.md — mark each answered there with its decision and J122, write the combined build order it records into §16 and INDEX.md's plan, and apply g item 112 like item 111; only the Fable review stays the owner's. (8) The document's one banner line under the base's title and each volume's marker line are the close's; do not add per-section markers. (9) ELEGANCE WITHOUT LOSING NUANCE (owner) — where appropriate, the set becomes simpler in FORM: one mechanism where two or more do the same job across the base and the volumes; one record where two describe the same thing; a table where scattered special cases are a table in disguise; a derivation where a stored value only repeats what can be derived (its lifecycle rows kept); a volume using the base's mechanism where it re-invented one; one pattern for the same kind of rule everywhere (the same shape for every soft pull, every existence chance, every ratchet, every closure state); names, units and record shapes made uniform. THE GUARD, absolute: elegance never removes or blurs a case, an exception, a distinction, a constant, a band row, a fallback, a failure rung, a provenance, a test bar, an owner rule or any behaviour; never merges two things that differ in any rule, value or lifecycle; never replaces a precise statement by a vague one ('etc.', 'as appropriate', 'similar rules apply'). Elegance means fewer, clearer mechanisms, never fewer words about the hard parts. Every elegance change is a canon ruling with an EQUIVALENCE LIST — each behaviour, case, bar and owner rule the original pieces carried and where it now lives — and a unification whose equivalence cannot be shown in full is not made (the pieces stay apart, and the ruling says what nuance separates them). Losing nuance for elegance is a blocker; a missed elegance is a minor. THE SHRINK GOAL, part of elegance (owner, 2026-09-29 ~02:25: "the appropriate shrink goal for the coherence pass which I intended to be part of the elegance piece I mentioned"): the set comes out MATERIALLY SHORTER, with a goal of about a third fewer words across the base and the volumes together (a band of 25 to 40 per cent), and every word removed must come from one of four sources only: RESTATEMENT (a fact stated again where a reference serves, principle (1)), NARRATION (how the text evolved, which review round added what, disposition talk — the validation records keep it), DUPLICATED MECHANISMS unified by an elegance ruling, and VERBOSE PROSE (the same content in fewer, plainer words). Never from content: no rule, case, value, bar, record field, lifecycle row, test, owner rule or reasoned decision is dropped or thinned to meet the goal; the goal yields to the guard every time. Each unit reports its words before and after and its share of each source; a unit shrinking by less than the band says what irreducible content holds it (not a defect); a unit shrinking by more than half is audited for nuance by its verifier before the loop may close. (10) AGREEMENT BETWEEN VOLUMES (owner, 2026-09-28 ~23:10: "make sure the coherence pass covers: Agreement between volumes") — every two units that meet, two volumes or a volume and a base part, agree on every subject they share: the same name, type, unit, value, enumeration, table and columns, stage order and inputs, event meaning, lifecycle rows (create, read, persist, regenerate, undo, migrate), tool and API shape, test id and bar, and build-plan dependency. Each shared subject has one canonical home (principle (1)); the canon's AGREEMENT MATRIX lists every one; every hand-off a volume lists for another (its Fix list, Integration notes, cross-volume hand-off rows, the volumes integrator's decisions and seam reports) is closed at its destination; a disagreement is ruled by principle (3)'s precedence and fixed at its home, never averaged or papered over. Seam checkers verify agreement in every full sweep. (11) EVERY EDGE DEFINED (owner, 2026-09-29 ~00:00: "add it", on the proposal to sweep edge cases across the whole architecture) — for every mechanism, the extremes of its inputs have a DEFINED OUTCOME, a FALLBACK on its failure ladder (§5: check, repair locally, retry that piece, simpler shape, substitute, leave out and report — never a whole-map retry) and a TEST: no input, one item, the maximum and the caps, degenerate geometry (zero-length, zero-area, self-touching, collinear, coincident), missing, empty or contradictory data (the dossier included), every rung from thorp to the metropolis cap, every toggle and preset (the parity presets, every layer on and off), and save, load, undo, redo, regenerate and migrate at each. The same holds for the COMBINATIONS where two units' features meet at their limits (a settlement that grows over a sealed void, a catacomb growing into a sewer, a raised way under an island's shade or over a harbour mouth, a deck plot on a bridge over a moat, a retaining wall on a cliff edge, a dossier removing what an edit pinned, an epoch undone after a save). An edge whose outcome is a crash, a hang, lost data, a silent wrong result or a whole-map retry is a BLOCKER; an undefined or untested edge is a MAJOR.
THE BAR IS ZERO (owner, 2026-09-29: "Can you make sure that you just get them down to zero findings at the end of this for each volume and the base"): the pass ends only when the base and EVERY volume hold zero open findings of any severity — every finding FIXED in the text or ruled INVALID by the adjudicator with its reason (the owner's no-defer rule: FIX or INVALID, nothing parked); no fix list may keep an open item. FINDING STANDARD: a finding names a concrete defect against these principles — a lost or changed fact, a conflict, a wiring or agreement gap, an undefined or untested edge, a broken reference or id, nuance lost, ambiguous or contradictory normative text, deferral wording; taste and preference are not findings. A finding ruled INVALID in any ${DIR}/adjudication-r*.md is closed for good: never raise it again. WORKING RULES: write your files incrementally (skeleton first, a PROGRESS ledger at the top); SAVE OFTEN (owner, 2026-09-29: "regularly save progress so in case another interruption happens, we can assure smooth resume"): save your working file after every section, block or checked batch, and append a CHECKPOINT line (what is done, what is next) to docs/lane-events.log at least every twenty minutes of work; SAVE YOUR RETURN: just before you log LANDED, write the exact structured result you are about to return to ${DIR}/results/<your lane key with every / replaced by _>.json (make the folder), so that under the RESUME RULE you return that file's content VERBATIM — a resumed run then receives the identical result and every later brief stays identical; RESUME RULE: if your output exists AND docs/lane-events.log has a LANDED line for your lane key, return the saved result file's content verbatim (or, where none exists, the summary from your output) without redoing; CONTINUE RULE: if it exists without one, continue from the first unfinished part. KILL-SAFE: the session may be cut at any moment. Redirect long output to files under ${SCRATCH}/ (make it) and grep them; never print whole files; read whole sections in ranges, never samples. Only the Assemble seat writes docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, the rationale file and the volume files. Checkpoint at about 300k tokens. When complete: echo "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M') <lane key> LANDED <files>" >> ${ROOT}/docs/lane-events.log  and return the structured summary.`

const INV_SCHEMA = { type: 'object', properties: { key: { type: 'string' }, files: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } }, facts: { type: 'number' }, terms: { type: 'number' }, restatements: { type: 'number' }, internal_conflicts: { type: 'number' }, broken_references: { type: 'number' } }, required: ['key', 'files', 'facts', 'terms', 'restatements', 'internal_conflicts', 'broken_references'] }
